{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3268","artifact_type":"architect","name":"plan/architect-groom-swarm-dispatch-contract.md@sha256:377bcd085628553e12f6cc132eb8ec16fedac81d77e116d2b9991b44734bbd3f"},"owner_reference":"3268","content":"# Architecture \u2014 Groom-Swarm Dispatch Contract Consistency (#3268)\n\n**Artifact type**: architect (WHAT the text must say \u2014 not the final diff)\n**Scope**: four textual edits across four dh markdown files, plus one placement correction\n(explained below) that adds a small section to a fifth file already in the dispatch-contract\nfamily. No code, no tests required by this spec.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Placement decision (resolves feature-context Q1\u2013Q3) \u2014 READ FIRST\n\nThe dispatch brief for this item asked for the new \"don't paste, don't speculate\" rule to live\nentirely inside `subagent-contract/SKILL.md`. That file is **agent-facing**: every existing\nsection (`<status>`, `<result_destination>`, `<reporting>`) is written in second person to the\n*dispatched* agent, and it is loaded only via each agent's own `skills:` frontmatter \u2014 an\norchestrator authoring a dispatch prompt never loads it. `plugins/development-harness/AGENTS.md:266-268`\nstates the counter-principle directly: \"Dispatch behaviour is defined in the `dispatch-contract`\nskill \u2026 so the decision belongs there and nowhere else.\" Putting an orchestrator-facing rule in\n`subagent-contract` places it exactly where AGENTS.md says it must not go, and where the party who\nneeds it (the orchestrator) will never read it \u2014 this is feature-context Risk R2, \"confirmed live\nin the item.\"\n\nThe three sub-rules split cleanly by audience:\n\n| Sub-rule | Audience | Home |\n|---|---|---|\n| (b) an agent with `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog` access self-fetches, does not wait for pasted content | the dispatched agent | `subagent-contract/SKILL.md` (matches its existing second-person voice) |\n| (a) a dispatch prompt carries item reference + genuinely dynamic cross-agent findings only | the orchestrator | `dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` |\n| (c) an orchestrator never writes a speculative conclusion into a dispatch prompt | the orchestrator | `dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` |\n\nThis spec therefore defines text for **five** files, not four: the four named in the dispatch, plus\na new orchestrator-facing section in `plugins/development-harness/skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md`.\n`dispatch-contract` is already loaded by `implement-feature`, `multi-perspective-review`,\n`task-worker`, `alignment-analyst`, and `review-synthesizer` per the feature-context stakeholder\ntable, so this is the smallest change that both respects AGENTS.md and reaches an audience that\nalready loads the file for dispatch decisions. Reachability for the groom workflow specifically is\nclosed by file 2 below (`swarm.md`), which now names `dh:dispatch-contract` directly in the\norchestrator-facing prose it already carries \u2014 no new skill-load wiring outside the four originally\nnamed files is required.\n\nThis is a deliberate deviation from the literal dispatch brief, made because the authoritative\nfeature-context artifact for this item raises this exact placement question as **blocking** (Q1)\nand marks the brief's originally-assumed placement as a confirmed risk (R2). See the `<concerns>`\nblock in the task-worker's response for the full citation trail.\n\n---\n\n## 1. `plugins/development-harness/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md`\n\n### 1a. Replace the `## Input` section (current lines 48\u201353)\n\nCurrent text lists literal content fields (item title, item description, research questions,\nRT-ICA summary). Replace with the item_ref/selector-only pattern used by `impact-analyst.md:22-24`,\n`classifier.md:15-20`, and `alignment-analyst.md:19-21`:\n\n```markdown\n## Input\n\n- **item_ref**: the backlog item reference (`#N`, title substring, beads nanoid, or URL)\n- **RT-ICA summary** (optional): pre-computed RT-ICA assessment from the orchestrator or the\n  `rtica-assessor` teammate \u2014 when present in the dispatch, use it directly and skip Step 0's own\n  pass; focus discovery on filling MISSING conditions and validating DERIVABLE ones instead.\n```\n\n### 1b. New self-fetch step, inserted immediately before the existing \"Step 0 - RT-ICA Assessment\"\n\nModeled on `impact-analyst.md:24-35` (fetch, then extract, then \"use this as the seed \u2014 do not\nbegin from memory or assumptions\"):\n\n```markdown\n### Step 0.0 - Fetch the Item\n\nCall `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_view(selector=item_ref, summary=False)` to fetch the full\nitem. Do not begin grooming from memory, from assumptions, or from anything other than this\nresponse \u2014 always fetch the item first.\n\nExtract from the response:\n\n- `title`, `description` \u2014 the primary grooming input\n- Any `**Hypothesis**:` lines inside the description \u2014 treated as research questions\n- Whichever of these sections earlier swarm waves have already written, if present: `Impact\n  Radius`, `Fact-Check`, `Issue Classification`, `Root-Cause Analysis`, `RT-ICA`, `Design Intent\n  Alignment`, `Research`\n\nFetch immediately before Step 0's RT-ICA pass, not earlier in the run, so the read reflects\nwhatever the rest of the swarm has written by the time the groomer \u2014 which always runs last \u2014\nactually starts. A read taken any earlier risks grooming against a stale Impact Radius or RT-ICA\nsection that a peer teammate revises after your fetch (the same staleness risk `rtica-assessor.md:90-99`\nguards against with its own late re-read).\n```\n\nThis also resolves feature-context Risk R6 (pasted content going stale between dispatch and use)\nas a side effect: self-fetch happens once, at the moment of use, instead of being frozen at\ndispatch time.\n\n---\n\n## 2. `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/swarm.md`\n\n### 2a. Replace the \"Groomer prompt\" section (current lines 255\u2013274)\n\nKeep the scope-boundary and description/AC-separation subsections (lines 259\u2013265) unchanged \u2014 only\nthe \"Input to groomer\" paragraph and its lead-in change:\n\n```markdown\n## Groomer prompt\n\nThe groomer agent receives all prior teammate output and produces groomed subsections.\n\n#### Scope boundary\n\nProblem space and outcomes only. Do NOT include implementation steps, architecture decisions, code design, or proposed solutions. Acceptance criteria must be observable checks \u2014 not implementation steps.\n\n#### Description / AC separation\n\nDescription is the problem statement. Acceptance Criteria are verifiable success conditions. Do not restate description inside ACs.\n\nGroomer agent: `subagent_type=\"dh:backlog-item-groomer\"`, model=sonnet.\n\nInput to groomer: `item_ref` only, per `dh:dispatch-contract`. The groomer fetches title,\ndescription, and every section prior swarm waves wrote (Impact Radius, Fact-Check, Issue\nClassification, Root-Cause Analysis, RT-ICA, Design Intent Alignment, Research) itself \u2014 see\n`backlog-item-groomer.md` Step 0.0. Do not paste any of those sections into the dispatch prompt.\n\nOrchestrator: before dispatching the groomer, verify your prompt names all required subsections: Reproducibility, Priority, Impact, Benefits, Expected Behavior, Acceptance Criteria, Files, Resources, Dependencies, Effort. A prompt that omits a subsection produces a missing section that cannot be recovered by retry alone.\n```\n\nThe `per dh:dispatch-contract` reference is the file's reachability fix: it points the orchestrator\nexecuting this workflow document at the skill carrying the full orchestrator-facing rule (file 4\nbelow), without requiring a separate frontmatter/skill-load change to `work-backlog-item/SKILL.md`\n\u2014 out of scope for this fix per the dispatch brief's four-file bound.\n\n### 2b. Replace sequence-diagram spawn lines\n\nCurrent diagram lines (content quoted from the file as read, not by literal line number \u2014 the\nMermaid block shifts if 2a's prose edit lands first):\n\n```text\n    O->>IA: spawn (item details, Files, Evidence, suggested_location)\n    O->>FC: spawn (item claims to verify, including any description Hypothesis line + Research section as prior context if available)\n    O->>RT: spawn (item details \u2014 waits for IA + FC)\n    O->>CL: spawn (item description)\n```\n\nReplace with:\n\n```text\n    O->>IA: spawn (item_ref)\n    O->>FC: spawn (item claims to verify, including any description Hypothesis line + Research section as prior context if available)\n    O->>RT: spawn (item_ref \u2014 waits for IA + FC)\n    O->>CL: spawn (item_ref)\n```\n\nOnly the `IA`, `RT`, and `CL` lines change (dispatch brief's \"~65/67/68\"). The `FC` line is\nintentionally left as-is \u2014 `fact-checker` is the accepted claim-scoped exception (feature-context\n\u00a72 outcome 3); its dispatch content is genuinely dynamic per-run data, not item content it could\ninstead fetch by reference.\n\n**Additional line the dispatch brief did not name, found during this design pass:** the same\nsequence diagram has a later line \u2014\n\n```text\n    O->>AA: spawn (item design intent \u2014 waits for IA)\n```\n\n\u2014 which is the identical inconsistency (pasted paraphrase instead of `item_ref`) for\n`alignment-analyst`, a confirmed self-fetching teammate (`alignment-analyst.md:19-21,100`). Leaving\nit unfixed reintroduces, one line down, the exact contradiction this item exists to remove. Replace\nwith:\n\n```text\n    O->>AA: spawn (item_ref \u2014 waits for IA)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. `plugins/development-harness/skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md`\n\nAdd one new tagged section, in the same style and voice as the existing `<status>` /\n`<result_destination>` / `<reporting>` sections. Place it after `<status>` and before\n`<result_destination>` (dispatch input logically precedes where you put your output):\n\n```markdown\n<dispatch_input>\n\nIf your `tools:` list carries `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog`, fetch the backlog item yourself \u2014\n`backlog_view(selector=<item_ref>, ...)` \u2014 instead of expecting its title, description, or any\nother section pasted into your dispatch prompt. Content addressable by an item reference and a\nsection name is never re-typed into a prompt; a dispatch naming only `item_ref` (or `selector`)\nalready gives you everything the item itself carries.\n\nThis does not cover content that exists only because the current run produced it \u2014 a claim string\nto verify, a finding a peer teammate computed this run \u2014 since no `item_ref` lookup retrieves\nsomething not yet written anywhere. A dispatch carrying that kind of content is not a violation of\nthis rule.\n\n</dispatch_input>\n```\n\nKeep this section narrow \u2014 agent-facing, additive, no orchestrator-facing content (that lives in\nfile 4). This is the only change to this file; it is loaded by 28 agents in\n`plugins/development-harness/agents/` plus one in `python3-development` (measured by\n`grep -rl 'dh:subagent-contract'`; the dispatch brief's \"25\" figure is stale \u2014 see the\n`<concerns>` block), so the addition must not alter the meaning of any existing section.\n\n---\n\n## 4. `plugins/development-harness/skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` (new file in scope \u2014 see \u00a70)\n\nAdd one new tagged section, in the same style as the existing `<dispatch_selection>` and\n`<artifact_consumption>` sections:\n\n```markdown\n<dispatch_prompt_scope>\n\nA dispatch prompt for an agent that can reach the backlog item's own content (see that agent's\n`dh:subagent-contract` `<dispatch_input>` rule) carries an item reference and whatever a peer\nteammate computed this run that no `item_ref` lookup can retrieve \u2014 never content pasted from the\nitem's own sections.\n\nNever write a speculative conclusion, a pre-formed verdict, or hedged language (\"likely\",\n\"probably\", \"seems\") into a dispatch prompt for an agent whose value is independent judgement.\n`\"this is very likely `defect` \u2014 confirm this classification yourself\"` is a violation: it hands\nthe receiving agent a conclusion to ratify, not a problem to classify. Give the agent the item\nreference and let it reach its own verdict.\n\n</dispatch_prompt_scope>\n```\n\nThe worked bad example is the actual text from the originating incident (feature-context \u00a71.3),\nquoted verbatim so a reader can hold real dispatch text against the rule and get a clear verdict \u2014\nthis directly satisfies the dispatch brief's \"specific enough to flag the classifier-anchoring\nincident\" requirement, in the file where an orchestrator will actually encounter it.\n\n---\n\n## 5. `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/close/start.md`\n\n### Replace Step 5.6 (current lines 105\u2013129)\n\nCurrent text has the orchestrator parse `Acceptance Criteria` out of its own `backlog_view` call,\nthen re-type each criterion into the `dh:task-worker` dispatch prompt. Replace item 4 (the\norchestrator-side extraction) and item 5 (the spawn) with a single spawn step that hands the agent\na reference instead of re-typed text \u2014 `dh:task-worker` carries full tool access, including\n`mcp__plugin_dh_backlog`, so it can fetch the section itself:\n\n```markdown\n4. Spawn a verification agent with subagent_type=\"dh:task-worker\". Prompt must include: `item_ref`\n   (the same selector used in Step 5.2), `section=\"Acceptance Criteria\"`, plan address (e.g.,\n   `P{id}`), and checklist status (100%). Instruct the agent to: call\n   `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_view(selector=item_ref, sections=[\"Acceptance Criteria\"])` and\n   parse each `-` line as a separate criterion itself, read the plan via\n   `mcp__plugin_dh_sam__sam_plan(plan=\"{address}\", config={\"action\": \"read\"})`, search\n   `git log --oneline -20`, check relevant files for each criterion, and return per-criterion\n   PASS/FAIL with file:line evidence. Do not parse or re-type the criteria text into the dispatch\n   prompt \u2014 the agent has backlog MCP access and fetches the item's Acceptance Criteria section\n   itself. Required return format:\n\n   ```text\n   [PASS] {criterion} \u2014 verified at {file}:{line} (or commit {sha})\n   [FAIL] {criterion} \u2014 {gap description}\n   Overall: PASS or FAIL (N/M criteria met)\n   ```\n\n   **If no acceptance criteria exist**: the agent's own `backlog_view` call returns an empty or\n   absent `Acceptance Criteria` section \u2014 instruct it in the prompt to warn \"No **Acceptance\n   Criteria**: field found \u2014 falling back to description-based verification\" and verify against the\n   item description instead.\n```\n\nImplementation note for whoever executes this: the current file numbers this step's two\nsub-items 4 (extract) and 5 (spawn); this replacement collapses them into one item 4, so the\nsubsequent items in Step 5.6 (\"Parse the agent verdict\", \"Collect agent verdict\" \u2014 currently 6 and\n7) renumber to 5 and 6. No wording change is needed in those two items themselves.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Verification approach for the reference-pattern files\n\nThis is a read/diff check confirming the new `subagent-contract` `<dispatch_input>` rule (\u00a73)\ndoesn't conflict with wording already in the files whose pattern this fix is copying. No edits.\n\n**Files to check:** `impact-analyst.md`, `classifier.md`, `alignment-analyst.md`, and\n`rtica-assessor.md` \u2014 the dispatch brief named the first three; `rtica-assessor.md` is added here\nbecause it is a fifth already-compliant self-fetching teammate the originating item's evidence\ntable omitted (feature-context Q5), and it costs nothing extra to include in a read-only check.\n\nFor each file:\n\n1. Confirm its `## Input` section still lists only `selector`/`item_ref` (plus, where applicable,\n   an optional pre-computed upstream result) \u2014 no literal content fields.\n2. Confirm its Phase 1 / Step 1 fetch step still calls `backlog_view` and still contains language\n   equivalent to \"do not begin from memory or assumptions \u2014 always fetch first.\"\n3. Confirm neither file's wording implies the orchestrator supplies item content \u2014 e.g. no phrase\n   like \"the orchestrator provides the description\" appears anywhere in the file.\n\nReport PASS with `file:line` citation for each of the three checks per file, or CONFLICT with the\nspecific contradicting line if step 1\u20133 wording has drifted since this spec was written.\n\n**`fact-checker.md` gets a different check, not the same one.** It is the accepted exception, not\na fifth instance of the self-fetch pattern \u2014 do not check it for `backlog_view` usage. Instead,\nconfirm that the new `<dispatch_input>` exception clause in \u00a73 (\"content that exists only because\nthe current run produced it\") actually covers `fact-checker.md`'s `CLAIM` /\n`SOURCE_FILE` / `PRIMARY_SOURCE` / `VERIFICATION_METHOD` / `FALSIFICATION_CRITERIA` input block\n(`fact-checker.md:45-55`) without requiring a rewrite. If a reader could plausibly read the new\nrule as demanding `fact-checker` self-fetch the item too, the exception clause's wording needs to\nbe sharpened \u2014 report that as a CONFLICT against \u00a73 rather than against `fact-checker.md`.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Explicitly out of scope for this fix (carried forward, not resolved here)\n\n- **Feature-context Q6** \u2014 whether the orchestrator supplying `fact-checker` with\n  `PRIMARY_SOURCE`/`VERIFICATION_METHOD` (`fact-checker.md:52-53`) counts as a second, unexamined\n  anchoring surface (choosing which source will confirm/refute). Real question, but it is about\n  `fact-checker.md`'s own content, which the dispatch brief did not name as a file to edit, and\n  answering it would widen this narrowly-scoped fix. Flagged for a follow-up item, not resolved\n  here.\n- **Feature-context Q7 / R5** \u2014 whether the orchestrator-facing anti-speculation half (\u00a74) needs a\n  mechanical guard (e.g. extending `test_subagent_contract_declaration_drift.py`, or a new grep-based\n  check that agent `## Input` sections list no literal content fields). The self-fetch half (\u00a73) is\n  a static, testable property of files; the anti-speculation half is a property of prompt text\n  generated at runtime that no test-at-rest can observe. This spec does not propose new test code \u2014\n  the dispatch brief scoped this to four (now five) markdown files, no executable code.\n"}